a postfix ++ through parentheses is applied before the dereference, not after.

EXPERIMENT-zzzw closed `(p)++`. This closes the rest of case 119: `*(p)++`,
which micro-c read as `(*p)++` -- a different program.

    p = buf + 8;  c = *(p)++;

    c == 8          correct
    p == buf + 8    WRONG, the pointer never moved
    buf[8] == 9     WRONG, the increment landed on the byte

C reads `*(p)++` as `*((p)++)`, because a postfix operator binds tighter than
unary `*`. The increment applies to p and the dereference applies to what the
increment RETURNED.

THE COMPILER ALREADY KNEW HOW TO DO THIS. `*p++` -- no parentheses -- is
correct and always was, and the emitted code says exactly why:

    lea_rax,[r13-8]            address of p
    # postfix inc/dec
    mov_rax,[rax]              load p
    push_rax                   keep the OLD p
    add_rax,BYTE '01'          p + 1
    mov_[rbx],rax              store it back
    pop_rax                    the old p again
    movzx_rax,BYTE_PTR_[rax]   THE STAR, applied after the increment
    # postfix inc/dec end

The postfix handler applies whatever num_dereference_after_postfix is still
carrying, and it applies it LAST. That is the whole of the correct order.

WHAT THE PARENTHESISED PATH DID INSTEAD. It walked the dereference itself,
before the increment, and then set num_dereference_after_postfix to 0 -- so the
postfix handler received p's VALUE where it expects an address, incremented the
byte at it, and had no star left to apply:

    lea_rax,[r13-8]            address of p
    mov_rax,[rax]              LOAD p -- the paren path's own walk
    # postfix inc/dec
    movzx_rax,BYTE_PTR_[rax]   treats p's value as the variable
    add_rax,BYTE '01'
    mov_[rbx],al               stores the byte back
    # postfix inc/dec end      no dereference, the count was cleared

So the fix is to spend nothing and hand the star on: return with the count
intact and let the one handler that knows the right order do both halves. Two
edits, and the second is what makes the first possible.

  THE PARENTHESES ARE THE TARGET, so primary_expr must leave p's ADDRESS
  rather than its value. assign_after_parens already arranges that for `=` and
  compound assignment, under a guard that the enclosing expression has no
  pending stars -- correct for `*(t) = v`, where the star is OUTSIDE the
  assignment and t really must be loaded and followed. A postfix ++/-- is the
  opposite: it binds tighter than the star, so the parentheses are the target
  whatever is pending. The two are separated rather than sharing a condition.

  THE DEREFERENCE WALK IS SKIPPED, by returning before it when a postfix
  follows. paren_lvalue is left exactly as it was and still covers the OTHER
  spelling, `(*p)++`, where the parentheses are around the dereference and the
  increment really does apply to it.

WHAT WAS TRIED AND DID NOT WORK, because the comment at that site claims
otherwise. Removing the `paren_lvalue = TRUE` branch for postfix -- the line
that is what makes `*(p)++` mean `(*p)++` -- does not fix it, it SEGFAULTS:
without it the dereference loads and the increment stores through the loaded
value. The order has to be corrected, not the flag.

WHERE tcc WRITES IT, the other half of the pair zzzs closed:

    #define dwarf_read_1(ln,end) ((ln) < (end) ? *(ln)++ : 0)   tcc.h:1859

tcc_eh_frame_hdr walks .eh_frame with dwarf_read_1 and dwarf_read_4 together.
zzzs stopped the segfault; this stops the walk seeing the same byte forever.

